1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Left: the caregiver's iPhone.
Right: the patient's iPad.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:05,000
One app, two roles.

3
00:00:05,000 --> 00:00:09,000
The notice comes first: SideBell cannot replace
emergency medical alert services.

4
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:13,000
Bluetooth is the only permission needed
for the core function.

5
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:16,000
Caregiver on the left.
Notifications let calls reach the lock screen.

6
00:00:16,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Patient on the right.
No account, no sign-up, no internet.

7
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:21,000
Connected over Bluetooth.
Both indicators turn green.

8
00:00:21,000 --> 00:00:25,000
The patient taps "Discomfort".
[alert sound] [spoken aloud: Discomfort]

9
00:00:25,000 --> 00:00:27,000
"Got it" — and the patient sees it acknowledged.
They never have to wonder if they were heard.

10
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:31,000
A non-urgent call: "Water".
[alert sound] [spoken aloud: Water]

11
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:33,000
Acknowledged again.
Every step lands within two seconds.

12
00:00:33,000 --> 00:00:35,000
Now the caregiver's phone
goes to the background.

13
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:39,000
The patient calls again.
[notification] [alert sound] [spoken aloud: Discomfort]

14
00:00:39,000 --> 00:00:43,000
Tapping the notification opens the call.
Acknowledged — without the app ever being on screen.
